---
/** About the archive: the old site's text, with a note on the full-text search. */
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { site } from "../config/site";
import { getIssues } from "../lib/issues.mjs";

const issues = getIssues();
const first = issues[0]!;
const last = issues.at(-1)!;
---

<Layout
  title="About"
  description="How and why The Daily Californian is digitizing its print archive from microfilm."
  breadcrumbs={[
    ["Archive", "/"],
    ["About", "/about"],
  ]}
>
  <div class="row">
    <div class="col-md-8">
      <h1>About the archives</h1>
      <p>
        The Daily Californian has been published continually since 1871, providing a rich historical
        record not only of campus life, but also of the world seen through the eyes of the
        undergraduates at one of the nation's foremost public universities. Our archives document
        the history of the Free Speech Movement, anti-war rallies such as the People's Park protest
        of 1971, and still-memorable Big Games against Stanford University. Taken together, they
        encompass a major piece of California and East Bay history.
      </p>
      <p>
        But most of these issues aren't easily accessible to the public. They're stored on microfilm
        reels in the Bancroft Library's University Archives. Additionally, the only microfilm
        readers available to us are also located at the University Archives, which necessitates that
        we do all of our archiving work on site. Moreover, the reels are neither searchable nor
        tagged.
      </p>
      <p>
        Our goal is to digitize and sort every Daily Californian microfilm reel in the library.
        While private companies often scan microfilm in bulk, this process can cost hundreds of
        thousands of dollars — money that would be better spent improving our current newspaper
        coverage. In lieu, we have staff members who are willing to dedicate their time to the
        manual process of scanning the original microfilm.
      </p>
      <p>
        We started with reels from 1964, the year of the Free Speech Movement, because the Daily
        Californian archives offer fascinating insight into that tumultuous year. This archive now
        holds {issues.length.toLocaleString("en-US")} issues, from {first.shortLabel} to{" "}
        {last.shortLabel}.
      </p>
      <h2>Searching the archive</h2>
      <p>
        Every page has been transcribed with optical character recognition, so you can{" "}
        <a href="/search">search the text</a> of every issue. The transcriptions are automatic and
        contain errors, especially in the oldest issues, so the scans themselves are always the
        authoritative record.
      </p>
      <p>
        UC Berkeley Library has also digitized much of the Daily Californian's history, which you
        can browse in <a href={site.libraryUrl}>its digital collections</a>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Questions, comments or corrections? Email{" "}
        <a href={`mailto:${site.contactEmail}`}>{site.contactEmail}</a>. This project is open-source
        on <a href={site.githubUrl}>GitHub</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
